In JavaScript, you handle exceptions (errors) using a `try...catch` statement. Code that might throw an error goes in the `try` block, and the error is handled in the `catch` block.

### Basic example
```javascript
try {
  // Code that might throw an error
  const result = riskyOperation();
  console.log(result);
} catch (error) {
  // Handle the error
  console.error("Something went wrong:", error.message);
}
```

### With `finally`
The optional `finally` block always runs (whether an error occurred or not), which is useful for cleanup:
```javascript
try {
  // Risky code
  openFile();
} catch (error) {
  console.error("Failed:", error);
} finally {
  // This always executes
  closeFile();
}
```

### Throwing your own errors
You can throw errors explicitly with `throw`:
```javascript
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) {
    throw new Error("Division by zero is not allowed");
  }
  return a / b;
}

try {
  console.log(divide(10, 0));
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.message); // "Division by zero is not allowed"
}
```

### Notes
- The `catch` binding (`error` above) receives an `Error` object (or a value of any type if something else was thrown). Common properties include `name`, `message`, and `stack`.
- In modern JavaScript you can also use `catch` without a binding if you don’t need the error object: `catch { ... }`.
- For asynchronous code, use `try...catch` with `async/await`, or handle rejections with `.catch()` on promises.

This is the standard, idiomatic way to catch exceptions in JavaScript.
